The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (substream->stream == SNDRV_PCM_STREAM_PLAYBACK) {
  graph = q6apm_graph_open(dai->dev, NULL, dai->dev, graph_id);
  ...
  dai_data->graph[graph_id] = graph;
}

Reason:
When `prepare` is called multiple times (e.g., after a stop that leaves the graph open but sets `is_port_started` to false), the old code opens a new graph without checking if one already exists. This results in multiple open graph instances without proper cleanup, causing memory leaks.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (substream->stream == SNDRV_PCM_STREAM_PLAYBACK && dai_data->graph[dai->id] == NULL) {
  graph = q6apm_graph_open(dai->dev, NULL, dai->dev, graph_id);
  ...
  dai_data->graph[graph_id] = graph;
}

Reason:
The added NULL check ensures that a new graph is opened only when no graph is already open. This prevents duplicate opens and resource leaks, while also handling the case where an existing graph (from a previous cycle) can be reused.

